/-!
# The twin relation: the runnable twin refines the provable twin

Every Python function is emitted twice: the provable `f` (List, ℚ, pure monads) and the runnable
`f'rn` (Array, Float, IO). This file states what "the same program" means between the two and
supplies the rewrite rules that show it per program (`#twin_check`, `Twin/Check.lean`).

* `TwinRel β α` relates a runnable type `β` to its provable (canonical) type `α` by a bijection
  `up`/`down`. Identity on equal types; `Array β ↔ List α`; lifted through `Prod`/`MProd`/`Option`/
  `Except`; a user class `C'rn ↔ C` gets a field-wise instance generated by `#twin_check`. There is
  deliberately no `Float ↔ ℚ` instance: rounding makes that a refinement only up to the IEEE
  enclosure of `PastaLean.PyAPI.FloatInterval`, not an equation.
* `@[twin]` lemmas rewrite runnable terms into the provable world: each `pyArray*` op on `l.toArray`
  is the `List` op followed by `toArray`, Bool tests (`==`, `&&`, `!`) become `decide` of the Prop the
  provable twin branches on, and a loop whose state holds an `Array` is conjugated to a loop over the
  `List` state (`forIn_twin`). After `simp only [twin]`, the two twins coincide syntactically.
-/

/-! Layout: `Twin/Attr.lean` (the `@[twin]` simp set), `Twin/Relation.lean` (`TwinRel` and its
instances), `Twin/Bridge.lean` (the bridge lemmas), `Twin/Check.lean` (`twin_tac`, `#twin_check`),
`Twin/Table.lean` (the substitution table), `Twin/Tactic.lean` (`twin% f`, `twin_table`, `#twin_table`).
Paper-level account: `docs/trust.md`. -/
